---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CalculatorDisclaimer from '../../components/CalculatorDisclaimer.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import { calculatorWebPage } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a ton-mile and why does drilling line wear get measured in it?',
    a: 'One ton-mile is one ton of load moved one mile of travel — it captures both how heavy the load was and how far the line moved it, which together drive fatigue in the wire rope. API RP 9B tracks cumulative ton-miles against a service-life rating so a line gets cut and slipped before it fails, not after.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do drilling, coring, and short-trip ton-miles use different multipliers?',
    a: 'Each covers a different amount of round-trip work. Drilling trips (3×) assume a full round trip to change the bit and back. Coring trips (2×) come out of the hole slower and more carefully but do not need the same repeated connections. A short trip (1×, no multiplier) is a partial round trip, so it gets the plain depth-to-depth difference with no adjustment.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does buoyancy factor use 65.5, not the exact density of steel?',
    a: 'Steel works out to roughly 65.44 ppg, and 65.5 is the field-rounded constant almost everyone actually uses. The gap is under a tenth of a percent, but it is worth knowing about if a ton-mile figure will not reconcile exactly against a service company\'s own numbers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is setting-casing ton-miles calculated the same way as a round trip?',
    a: 'No — it is a separate, one-way calculation, because running casing to bottom is not a round trip. It still uses the same buoyed-weight logic, just applied to the casing string being run rather than the drill string being tripped.',
  },
];



const webAppLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/ton-mile#app",
  "name": "Ton-Mile Calculator: Drilling Line Service Life",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/ton-mile",
  "description": "Free ton-mile calculator for drilling line service life: round trip, drilling, coring, short trip, and setting-casing ton-miles per API RP 9B / Lapeyrouse.",
  "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
  "applicationSubCategory": "Drilling engineering calculator",
  "operatingSystem": "Any (web browser)",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isAccessibleForFree": true,
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" }
};

const webPageLd = calculatorWebPage(webAppLd, { lang: 'en' });

---
<BaseLayout
  title="Ton-Mile Calculator: Drilling Line Service Life"
  description="Free ton-mile calculator for drilling line service life: round trip, drilling, coring, short trip, and setting-casing ton-miles per API RP 9B / Lapeyrouse."
  extraJsonLd={[webAppLd, webPageLd]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }, { label: 'Ton-Mile', href: '/calculators/ton-mile' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators/ton-mile"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators/ton-mile" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Calculator</div>
    <h1>Ton-Mile <span>Calculator</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Round trip, drilling, coring, short trip, and setting-casing ton-miles for tracking drilling line service life against its API RP 9B rating.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="calc-box">
          <div class="calc-title">🎣 Rig &amp; String</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="mw">Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="mw" value="9.6" step="0.1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dpwt">Drill Pipe Weight, air (lb/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dpwt" value="13.3" step="0.1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="stand">Average Stand Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="stand" value="90">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="block">Travelling Block Weight (lb)</label>
              <input type="number" id="block" value="15000">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dclen">Drill Collar Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dclen" value="600">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="dcwt">Drill Collar Weight, air (lb/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="dcwt" value="83" step="0.1">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-title" style="margin-top:24px;">📏 Round Trip at Two Depths</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="deptha">Depth A — start of interval (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="deptha" value="8000">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="depthb">Depth B — end of interval (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="depthb" value="8500">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:16px;">
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Buoyancy Factor</span><span class="output-value" id="out-bf">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line headline"><span class="output-label">Round Trip TM @ Depth A</span><span class="output-value" id="out-rttma">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line headline"><span class="output-label">Round Trip TM @ Depth B</span><span class="output-value" id="out-rttmb">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Drilling / Connection TM (3× diff.)</span><span class="output-value" id="out-drilling">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Coring TM (2× diff.)</span><span class="output-value" id="out-coring">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">Short-Trip TM (straight diff.)</span><span class="output-value" id="out-shorttrip">—</span></div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-title" style="margin-top:24px;">🛢 Setting Casing (separate one-way calc)</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="csgwt">Casing Weight, buoyed (lb/ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="csgwt" value="40" step="0.1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="csgdepth">Casing Depth (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="csgdepth" value="8500">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="csgjoint">Length of One Joint (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="csgjoint" value="40">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:16px;">
            <div class="output-line headline" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">Setting Casing Ton-Miles</span><span class="output-value" id="out-csgtm">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <CalculatorDisclaimer lang="en" context="Track cumulative ton-miles against your drilling line's actual service-life rating and cut-and-slip programme, not a one-off figure from this page." />
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The Formulas Used</h2>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Buoyancy Factor</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>BF = (65.5 − MW) ÷ 65.5</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Round Trip Ton-Miles</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>RTTM = [Wp × D × (Ls + D) + 2D × (2×Wb + Wc)] ÷ (5280 × 2000)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Wp = buoyed drill pipe weight/ft · D = depth · Ls = stand length · Wb = block weight · Wc = buoyed collar weight minus equivalent drill pipe weight over the collar length. Per API RP 9B / Lapeyrouse.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Drilling, Coring &amp; Short-Trip Ton-Miles</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>Drilling = 3 × (RTTM at B − RTTM at A)<br />Coring = 2 × (RTTM at B − RTTM at A)<br />Short-Trip = RTTM at B − RTTM at A</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Setting Casing Ton-Miles</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>TM = [Wp × D × (Lj + D) + D × Wb] × 0.5 ÷ (5280 × 2000)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Wp = buoyed casing weight/ft · D = casing depth · Lj = length of one joint · Wb = block weight.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>Worked Example</h3>
        <p>9.6 ppg mud, 13.3 lb/ft drill pipe, 90 ft stands, 15,000 lb block, 600 ft of 83 lb/ft collars, round trip from 8,000 ft to 8,500 ft:</p>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Buoyancy Factor</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>(65.5 − 9.6) ÷ 65.5 = 0.8534</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block mt-24">
          <dt class="formula-label">Round Trip Ton-Miles at Each Depth</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>RTTM at 8,000 ft = 169.10<br />RTTM at 8,500 ft = 184.23</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block mt-24">
          <dt class="formula-label">Drilling Ton-Miles for This 500 ft Interval</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>3 × (184.23 − 169.10) = 45.4 ton-miles</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">A single 500 ft round trip at this depth costs the drilling line about 45 ton-miles of its service-life rating — the same figure the video above walks through.</dd>
        </dl>

        <p>Buoyancy factor here uses the same 65.5 constant as the <a href="/calculators/formulas-cheat-sheet">drilling formulas cheat sheet</a>'s buoyancy section — see that page if a figure needs reconciling to more decimal places.</p>

        <h2>Watch: One Trip, 45 Ton-Miles of Wear</h2>
        <VideoEmbed
          videoId="Z2-OjIqilNQ"
          title="One trip, 45 ton-miles of wear"
          description="One 500-foot round trip just cost your drilling line 45 ton-miles. Free calculator tracks drilling, coring, short-trip, and casing ton-miles separately. rigfloorhq.com/calculators/ton-mile"
          uploadDate="2026-10-18T20:03:01-07:00"
          duration="PT0M41S"
          thumbnailUrl="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/Z2-OjIqilNQ/hqdefault.jpg"
          lang="en"
        />

        <p>Tracking cumulative ton-miles against the drilling line's service life is equipment-integrity work a <a href="/careers/toolpusher">toolpusher</a> owns rig-wide — see the full toolpusher career guide for what the role involves and pays.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .output-line.headline .output-value { color: var(--amber); font-size: 18px; }
</style>

<script is:inline src="/scripts/calculators/ton-mile.js" defer></script>
